    {# ============ CLARITY — what counts against storage ============ #}
    <section class="section fade-up fade-up-5">
        <h2>What counts against storage?</h2>
        <p>Two meters, counted separately, and each is a <strong>total across all your vaults</strong> &mdash; not a per-vault limit. <strong>Notes</strong> is your writing (the text, tags, and links); <strong>Attachments</strong> is the files and audio you drop in. They don&rsquo;t share a bucket, so a plan with a small notes allowance can still hold plenty of attachments &mdash; and you can spend the budget on one big vault or spread it across several.</p>
        <p>Notes are just text, so a little goes a long way: <strong>100 MB is roughly 25,000&#8202;+&nbsp;notes</strong> &mdash; more than most people write in years. Standard&rsquo;s 250 MB is ~60,000, Plus&rsquo;s 500 MB ~125,000, and Power&rsquo;s 1 GB ~250,000. You&rsquo;ll almost certainly run out of things to write before you run out of room.</p>
        <p class="muted">Entry is notes-only &mdash; a plain-text vault, no attachments or voice. Every other plan meters both, plus a monthly pool of voice transcription.</p>
    </section>

    {# ============ NO LOCK-IN ============ #}
    <section class="section fade-up fade-up-6">
        <h2>No plan locks you in</h2>
        <p>Cancel anytime; export everything, anytime. Every note, tag, and link comes out as plain markdown with the structure intact &mdash; because a connection you can&rsquo;t leave isn&rsquo;t a relationship. See <a href="/guides/yours-to-keep/">Yours to keep</a> for how export works.</p>
    </section>

